# tests/T10.bd - T10 "Death trace: what the meter reads at the
# instant of the kill". First test of the KILL-TRIGGER block.
#
# PURPOSE
#   No run in this entire investigation has ever logged a get_cpu() reading
#   immediately before dying. Every crash point on record (+40.7s, +111.0s,
#   +40.3s, +65.5s, +64.3s ...) was INFERRED from whichever debug checkpoint
#   happened to print last. This script does nothing but print one numbered
#   CPU sample per line, unpaced, until the engine kills it.
#   THE UNPACED BURST *IS* THE EXPERIMENT. This is the one script in tests/
#   that is deliberately allowed to violate the "sleep() every 5-8 lines"
#   rule: the kill is the result, not a failure mode. Everything outside the
#   loop is paced normally.
#
# WHAT IT ANSWERS
#   1. What does get_cpu() actually read at (or one line before) death?
#   2. For how many consecutive REAL seconds was it at the ceiling first?
#      field_notes claims "100% for 5 straight seconds". Never measured.
#   3. Is get_cpu() even the quantity the killer integrates? test_decode_
#      pacing PART A sat at a LOGGED cpu=100 for ~840s and never died, which
#      already hints the answer is no.
#   4. What is the player's ACTUAL tick rate / CPU tier? Recorded nowhere in
#      this project - every calculation so far has ASSUMED base tier.
#   5. Does a killed script leave residual load for the next one? The
#      entry_cpu number in the header line is a direct readout.
#
# OUTPUT FORMAT
#   Header line:  KILLTRACE part=A entry_cpu=<c> floor_cpu=<c>
#     entry_cpu = get_cpu() on the 2nd executable line, before any settle.
#                 Cold run should be ~0 (test_cpu_decay saw exactly 0 here).
#                 A HOT run reading well above that = carryover is real.
#     floor_cpu = get_cpu() after sleep(3). This is the level the burst
#                 starts climbing from. Expect ~10 (the known idle floor).
#   Then one line per iteration, three space-separated numbers:
#       n  t  cpu
#     n   = iteration counter. FIRST on the line on purpose, so that even a
#           truncated or half-written final line still tells us where it died.
#     t   = whole seconds since the burst began (get_unix_time(), 0 ticks).
#     cpu = PART A: get_cpu() at that instant.
#           PART B: a copy of n (the control - see below).
#   Final line, ONLY if nothing killed it:
#       SURVIVED n=<n> t=<t> cpu=<c>
#   Its ABSENCE is unambiguous proof of death rather than a quiet exit.
#
# HOW TO READ THE PASTED OUTPUT
#   * LAST LINE = the meter's value at death, and the exact unpaced-line
#     count that proved fatal. This is the headline number.
#   * Lines sharing one t value = iterations per real second. The loop body
#     is 2 or 3 executed lines (depending on whether the `while` condition
#     line costs a tick - itself unknown), so:
#         3.3 iter/s -> base tier 10 ticks/s, while-line DOES cost a tick
#         5.0 iter/s -> base tier 10 ticks/s, while-line is FREE
#         6.7 / 10.0 -> Mk.II (20 t/s), and so on doubling per tier
#     Either way this is the first direct tier measurement in the corpus,
#     and the 3.3-vs-5.0 split resolves the loop-condition tick question
#     as a free bonus.
#   * Count how long the tail sat at the ceiling. Two ways, use both:
#       (a) number of DISTINCT t values whose cpu is at the ceiling
#           (coarse: integer seconds, +/-1s of bucket-boundary error)
#       (b) (number of SAMPLES at the ceiling) / (iterations per second)
#           (finer, sub-second - preferred)
#     If "100% for 5 straight seconds" is literally true, both land on 5.
#   * Comparing final n across repeat runs is the cheapest probe of the
#     central run-to-run-variance mystery that exists: a ~23-second
#     experiment instead of a 40-120s one.
#
# PARTS
#   PART A - the logged line includes get_cpu()      (instrumented arm)
#   PART B - the logged line substitutes str(n) for str(get_cpu())  (control)
#   The two logged expressions are structurally IDENTICAL: same three str()
#   calls, same two concatenations, same line count per iteration. The ONLY
#   difference is get_cpu() vs a plain variable read. The gap in final n
#   between A and B therefore MEASURES how much get_cpu() polling shortens
#   the fuse, instead of assuming it. (field_notes' "get_cpu() adds load"
#   correction currently rests on one unquantified observation and is
#   load-bearing for a third of this investigation.)
#   In a PART B paste, column 3 equals column 1 - that is how you tell the
#   arms apart at a glance.
#
# WHY THE LOOP IS TIME-BOUNDED, NOT COUNT-BOUNDED
#   A fixed iteration cap is tier-dependent: at Mk.IV/Mk.V a 150-iteration
#   burst finishes in under 2 real seconds, i.e. BEFORE the documented
#   5-second window could ever elapse, and the run would "survive" for
#   reasons having nothing to do with the kill predicate. The 20-second wall
#   clock bound is tier-independent and gives 4x margin over the documented
#   constant. The n < 400 clause is only a paste-volume safety valve; if a
#   run ends at exactly n=400 with t far below 20, that means a high CPU
#   tier - report it and we re-run with a bigger cap.
#
# HOW TO RUN (@param binds POSITIONALLY - no -flag= syntax)
#   Run FIRST THING in a fresh session. Confirm with `ps` and `jobs` that
#   nothing else is running.
#     1) T10 A   - wait for the overload banner, paste EVERYTHING
#        including the "[SYSTEM] CPU overloaded..." line.
#     2) idle ~30s, repeat. Three COLD PART A runs total.
#     3) Three more PART A runs BACK TO BACK, retyping within ~3 seconds of
#        each kill. Cold-vs-hot entry_cpu is the carryover measurement.
#     4) idle ~30s, then two PART B runs.
#   8 runs total, each ~23s of script time, ~5-6 minutes all in.
#   Record the final n of every run, and paste every run in full.
# @param part "A = log get_cpu() each line, B = control without it" default=A

var PART = str(get_param("part", "A")).replace("\"", "").trim()
var c0 = get_cpu()
sleep(3)
log("KILLTRACE part=" + PART + " entry_cpu=" + str(c0) + " floor_cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
var n = 0
var t0 = get_unix_time()
if PART == "A":
    while get_unix_time() - t0 < 20 and n < 400:
        n = n + 1
        log(str(n) + " " + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + " " + str(get_cpu()))
else:
    while get_unix_time() - t0 < 20 and n < 400:
        n = n + 1
        log(str(n) + " " + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + " " + str(n))
sleep(5)
log("SURVIVED n=" + str(n) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + " cpu=" + str(get_cpu()))
